# Row A — source-pack verification round

This round is the first in which the Bałaban source PDFs were actually
available in the project tree (`ym-balaban-source-pdfs-20260828.tar.gz`, with
`SHA256SUMS`).  Every statement below about the papers was read off the
extracted text of those files; nothing is quoted from memory, and no page or
equation number appears here that was not located in the extracted text.

## 0. What was located in the sources

**CMP 98 (Averaging operations for lattice gauge theories, 98 (1985) 17–51).**

* Eq. (124) — the full linear form `Q(V₀)A` of the one-step averaging operator,
  as a sum of a main term plus four correction terms built from the functions
  `g(∓i ad Γ)`, `g⁻¹(·)` and `e^{i ad Γ}`, each of which equals `1` at argument
  zero.
* Eq. (125) — the main term `Q₀ = Q_{V₀}`, exactly in the form quoted in the
  earlier round: a sum over `x ∈ B(c₋)` of `L^{−(d+1)}(R_{0,c₋}A)([x,x'])`.  The
  formula, the normalisation `L^{−(d+1)}`, and the summation range that earlier
  rounds had to take from the request text are therefore **confirmed against the
  source**.
* Eq. (126) and Proposition 3 — the linear estimate: the main term obeys
  `|(Q₀A)_c| ≤ |A|`, and the whole linear form obeys
  `|(Q(V₀)A)_c| ≤ (1 + O(1)L²α₀)|A|`, the correction terms being `O(L²α₀)`.
  The constant in `O(1)` is **not** given numerically; the paper's constants
  `C₁, α₀, c₃` are existentially quantified ("there exist constants … such that
  for α₀, α₁ sufficiently small"), with dependence on `d` and `L` recorded but
  no value.
* Proposition 7 — joint analyticity: for `U₀` in the stated regularity class and
  `U' = e^{iηA'}` with `|A'| < α₁`, and `α₀, α₁` sufficiently small, `Q_k(U'U₀, ηA)`
  is analytic in the complex variables `A', A`, with the earlier bounds holding
  uniformly in `A'`.  The **analytic radius is again an unspecified "sufficiently
  small" constant**; the source gives no numerical neighbourhood.

**CMP 109 (Renormalization group approach I, 109 (1987) 249–301).**

* Theorem 1 — the small-field effective-action package: *if* the sequence of
  effective coupling constants lies in an interval `]0, γ]` with `γ` sufficiently
  small, then the small-field effective actions have the stated form with
  irrelevant terms obeying the stated bound.  Proved there for arbitrary
  semisimple compact `G ⊂ U(N)` in `d = 4` (and `d = 3`).  The paper states
  explicitly that no asymptotic behaviour of the couplings is assumed.  This is
  the implication form in which the Lean weld consumes it.
* Theorem 2 — for `d = 4`, `G = SU(2)`, and `γ` sufficiently small: for
  sufficiently small `g` there exists a bare coupling `g₀ = g₀(ε, g)` such that
  the effective couplings stay in `]0, γ]`, `g_K = g` at the observation scale,
  and two-sided bounds of the form
  `1/g² + β log(L^k ε)^{−1} ≤ 1/g_k² ≤ 1/g² + β' log(L^k ε)^{−1}` hold with
  `0 < β ≤ β'`.
* §5, Eq. (5.1) — the vacuum polarization tensor is *defined* as the second
  variation `δ²/δB(b)δB(b') E^{(j)}(U_j(exp iB))` at `B = 0` (as a limit in the
  stated scaling), confirming the definition the polarization weld uses.
* §5, Eqs. (5.36)–(5.41) — the tensor-structure normalisation: in momentum space
  the polarization tensor is written as `β·(δ_{μν}Δ(p) − ∂_μ(p) conj ∂_ν(p))`
  plus a remainder that is a third-order polynomial in the lattice derivative
  symbols, the off-diagonal coefficients being all equal after the stated
  `2^{d−2}` normalisation, and `β` is expressed through the expansion
  coefficients of the effective action.

## 1. The two findings that matter for the frontier

**(F1) The tensor-structure normalisation is now available, and has been
formalised.**  Earlier rounds listed "the tensor-structure normalisation of the
polarization tensor" as a missing datum.  The source fixes it: the structure is
the lattice transverse one, `T_{μν}(p) = δ_{μν}Δ(p) − ∂_μ(p) conj ∂_ν(p)` with
`∂_μ(p) = e^{ip_μ} − 1`.  `RequestProject/YangMills/CMP109TensorNormalisation.lean`
formalises this and proves the resulting *convention-free* extraction identity:
the mixed momentum second derivative of the off-diagonal structure at zero
momentum equals `−1` (`latticeTransverse_offDiag_mixed_deriv`), hence for any
decomposition on that structure with a remainder whose mixed second derivative
vanishes at zero,

    β = − ∂²Π_{μν}/∂p_μ∂p_ν |_{p=0},   μ ≠ ν   (`beta_coefficient_extraction`).

So the coefficient is determined by the polarization tensor alone; no further
convention has to be assumed in the weld.

**(F2) The Row-A completion content is exactly CMP 109 Theorem 2 — and CMP 109
does not prove it.**  The Row-A completion contract asks for (i) a bilateral
cumulative beta tube with positive lower slope and (ii) a tuned bare coupling
keeping every effective coupling in the small-field window with a prescribed
nonzero coupling at the observation scale.  That *is* Theorem 2 of CMP 109,
including the two-sided form of the bounds.  The paper states the theorem and
then says its proof, based on perturbative calculations, will be given in a
separate paper.  That separate paper is not in the source pack, and the other
items in the pack (CMP 98, CMP 99, CMP 102, the two "Propagators and
renormalization transformations" papers, the three-dimensional UV-stability
paper and the supplement) do not contain it either.  Two further facts sharpen
this: Theorem 2 is stated only for `SU(2)` (the paper calls the restriction
superficial but does not remove it), and CMP 109 proves no positivity or lower
bound for the `β` it defines in §5 — positivity is part of the deferred
statement.

Consequence for the frontier: **the missing Row-A input cannot be supplied by
literal transcription from the pack**, because the source itself defers it.  It
has to be proved.

## 2. What was proved in Lean this round

All new modules build under `lake build RequestProject`, contain no `sorry`,
no `axiom`, no `@[implemented_by]`, and every new top-level theorem reports only
`propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`.

* `RowATerminalTube.lean` — Row A-i, the **defect-free** bilateral tube.  The
  additive defect `D` of the earlier cumulative bound is removed in the two
  regimes in which removal is actually true:
  - `terminal_tube_of_small_bare_coupling`: uniform in the block, once the bare
    inverse coupling satisfies `C ≤ ε u₀²`;
  - `terminal_tube_of_late_start`: for every tolerance `ε > 0` there is a scale
    `k₀` beyond which every block obeys the tube, with no smallness assumption
    at all.  The mechanism is the new telescoping estimate
    `debt_block_telescope`: the total quartic debt of a block `[k, m)` is at most
    `C·κ/u_k` with `κ = (u₀+b₊)/u₀`.
  Both give slopes `b_Z⁻ − ε > 0` and `b_Z⁺ + ε`, i.e. the absorption
  `b̃∓ = b∓ ∓ ε` asked for by the contract.

* `RowAShooting.lean` — Row A-ii, the sensitivity-to-tuning bridge.
  - `cumulative_sensitivity_le`: quartically suppressed per-shell sensitivities
    `L_j ≤ C_s g_j⁴` sum, **uniformly in the cutoff**, to `C_s κ b₋⁻¹ g₀²`;
    `cumulative_sensitivity_lt_one` records that this is `< 1` for a small
    enough bare coupling.  This is the `q < 1` object the shooting bridge
    consumes, and it is derived, not assumed, *given* the per-shell sensitivity.
  - `shooting_exists_unique`: `q < 1` makes the shooting map a contraction of
    `ℝ`, so the tuned bare coupling exists and is unique.
  - `tuned_bare_coupling_window`: the tuned trajectory hits the prescribed
    observation-scale value exactly, and every effective coupling on the way
    satisfies `0 < g_j² ≤ (u⋆ − b₊K)⁻¹`.

* `RowACompletion.lean` — the completion predicate `RowACompletion`, stated on a
  *single* generated trajectory (tuning, observation-scale value, window, and
  bilateral defect-free tube all for the same bare coupling), together with:
  - `rowA_completion_of_uniform_debt`, which inhabits it from the hypothesis
    package;
  - `quartic_debt_bootstrap`, which shows the per-shell debt tolerance in that
    package is a *consequence* of the quartic remainder bound on the physical
    region (bare couplings above a floor `v₀` with `C ≤ ε v₀²`), proved by a
    joint induction: the lower bound on `u_j` controls the remainder, and the
    controlled remainder keeps the shell coefficient nonnegative, propagating the
    lower bound;
  - `rowA_completion_witness`, an explicit numerical instance of the hypothesis
    package (with a genuinely coupling-dependent remainder), so the predicate is
    demonstrably inhabitable and the assembly theorem is not vacuous.

* `CMP109TensorNormalisation.lean` — finding (F1) above.

## 3. Honest status

Row A is **not** closed, and the frozen research count stays at four (A, B, C,
D).  The single missing input on the Row-A path is now completely isolated and
named:

> a quantitative sensitivity estimate for the Bałaban shell coefficient with
> respect to the bare coupling — in Lipschitz form, `|β_j(v) − β_j(w)| ≤ L_j|v − w|`
> with `Σ_{j<K} L_j ≤ q < 1` uniformly in the cutoff `K`.

Everything downstream of that estimate on Row A is now machine-checked here: the
contraction, the tuned bare coupling, the observation window, and the
defect-free bilateral tube on the same trajectory.  Upstream, the estimate is
